{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: archive row diagnostic line at exact head a99c2e6742012e2b51968725f11b43cdc5d99964, tree f80e14ef35b7a5aeae91772f8faf3e9f8d0397f6 (review request 09041612, promise 6a3f292a, implementation 57917747 / 352e2c80, delivered in the form planner decision 52d805cb chose). It may land. Deployment is planner-owned. Please read the residual notes below before the deploy.\n\nRead order: I read the primary, notes/implementation/2026-10-24-archive-operator-view.md (artifact a32c0d9e), first and in full. Then I read the whole diff from the merge-base with Main, 854d8fe8: archive-operator.ts (new), object.ts, archive-row.ts, archive-operator.test.ts (new), the seal file and docs/security/entry-points.md. The branch merges Main twice (920c9051 and the slice B head 854d8fe8 at 4ff21a11), records the gate at 8c802272 (tree 96e7e0f6) and adds only the note at a99c2e67 (27 insertions, 5 deletions). Merging the head into Main fe4967ca gives exactly its tree, f80e14ef. The diff touches seven paths, and the act log holds a pointer at this head for each of them.\n\nWhat the head does, against 52d805cb and the conditions of 57917747:\n- One fixed line per activation, before recovery. The constructor calls #diagnoseArchive() once, after #readSql (object.ts:180) and #name (object.ts:228) are set and before the production archive activation, which now waits for the diagnostic promise before committed() and wakes.set(). The promise is memoized on the instance, so repeated entry reads once and logs once. Every asynchronous ingress (#invoke, #publicInvoke, observed) awaits that promise before #recoverArchiveAlarm; the synchronous paths (#invokeSync, settle and the pinned readers through #first) queue one continuation behind it with the two coalescing flags, so recovery and first-turn work run after the line and are never lost.\n- Bounded, read-only, no new caller. archiveOperatorState reads the scope metadata and the archive row through the existing strict ArchiveRows decoder on a SQL facade with no transaction and no mutation, reads the stored wake index without repairing it, and reads the platform alarm once. The unused RPC from 03b40dfc is gone; nothing in the diff adds a route, method, member or MCP surface. The new storedWake reader bounds the row at 256 bytes and reports malformed as unavailable, not as absent.\n- Exact scope identity. A metadata scope that is not this object's name answers unknown with reason scope; the stored-name control below is the red for that fence.\n- Absent and unknown stay distinct, and nothing is synthesized: the witnesses damage the row, the wake and the scope record in turn and get unknown for the damaged part only.\n- No secrets, no free text. The line carries the scope id, phase, state, a reason restricted to the known reason codes (anything else becomes unknown), the confirmed and pending positions as commit and through, the stored wake and the alarm time. It omits the repository, the manifest, the stop detail and all error text. The witness stops a row with a private reason and detail and shows neither in the line or the view.\n- The synchronous SQL finishes inside the constructor before the single getAlarm await, which is what the failed gate 45227 asked for: the malformed-query witness in item-query-owned failed because the earlier source started the diagnostic inside queryItems, before input validation. The correction moves it to the constructor, and that witness passes in gate 94166 without any change to the query bounds.\n\nWitnesses. archive-operator.test.ts has ten cases. Five warm-view cases, one per phase (absent, target, confirmed, stopped, pending), trap every write path (driver run, wake and committed; every host method; target resolution; any SQL that is not a SELECT from meta; setAlarm, deleteAlarm and waitUntil), compare the full application SQLite snapshot and the alarm before and after, and check the view against the row the store itself loaded, including a deleted alarm under a persisted wake. One case shows an uncreated object stays absent and a dropped meta table reads unknown. Four cold-activation cases install the instrumentation before the object restarts, enter nativeBirth, readiness, queryItems and proofSource twice each, and assert the row read precedes the method body, one load and one line per activation across further calls, recovery after the line, the exact eight field names, the pending Mark position, and no manifest, credential, token or secret text. All ten pass in the gate (test.log lines 493157 to 494204).\n\nControls and earlier runs:\n- Stored-name control 41674 (/private/tmp/artroom-archive-operator-log-stored-name-control-evidence-20261024-v1): removing the scope fence fails all five stored-state cases at the unknown/scope assertion (5 failed, 5 passed). It ran on archive-operator.ts bytes with SHA-256 71ed84cd and restored them. That file and archive-row.ts are byte-identical between the controlled source 4fcc3e08 and this head, so the control carries without qualification; only object.ts and the test file changed after it.\n- The four cold-activation witnesses have no mutation control. By my reading each assertion fails without the behaviour: a missing constructor read leaves no diagnostic-row-read entry, a second read or line breaks the count of one, and recovery before the line breaks the order check. Nonblocking.\n- Gate 45227 at 4ff21a11 stays recorded as failed (1 failed, 3,540 passed, 3 skipped; result.json gatePassed false). The focused runs 20590, 96204 and 72970 are described in the note as they ran.\n\nGate 94166 at 8c802272 (tree 96e7e0f6), raw folder /private/tmp/artroom-archive-operator-log-gate-evidence-20261024-v2, audited in /private/tmp/artroom-archive-operator-log-gate-v2-independent-audit-20261024-v1 (manifest SHA-256 e4b8100f; its 3 payloads and all 28 pinned inputs match): result.json records expectedHead 8c802272, expectedTree 96e7e0f6, unchangedCheckpoint true, gatePassed true, exit 0; before.json and after.json are byte-identical with a clean status; stdout.log shows all eight stages exit 0. stages/test.log reads \"Test Files 497 passed | 1 skipped (498)\" and \"Tests 3541 passed | 3 skipped (3544)\"; the Node block reports pass 39 fail 0 and the Counting block pass 55 fail 0. The head differs from 8c802272 only in the note, so docs/testing.md:597 needs no rerun. The callback gaps (19 ready, 21 result, all in passing files) are a reporting limitation.\n\nDocument checks at a99c2e67:\n- git diff --check from 854d8fe8 is clean;\n- check-commit-messages from 854d8fe8 reports 14 checked, 0 flagged; the longest new subject is 64 characters;\n- section citations on an export report 1,877 resolved, 0 broken and 5 known unresolved; the citation script is identical to Main's;\n- merging the head into Main fe4967ca gives exactly its tree, f80e14ef.\n\nArchitecture: the observation lives in one module with no dependency on the driver, the host or authority, and the object owns exactly one call to it per activation. The cost is one bounded set of metadata reads and one getAlarm per activation, and one awaited settled promise per later call. The line is the planner's chosen interface, and the note and docs/security/entry-points.md say what it is and is not.\nSecurity: I found no defect. No route, caller, authority, credential or host I/O is added. The reader cannot write: the SQL trap, the alarm traps and the full snapshot comparison prove it for every phase. The line exposes archive commit hashes, positions and times that the planner already reads in archive-pass-diagnostic-v1, with reasons whitelisted and detail omitted. The object.ts and archive-row.ts seals change to the maintained parser's values and archive-operator.ts is sealed as a new owner; the entry-point Node block passed in the gate on identical bytes.\nSimplification: about 100 lines of reader, 60 of object wiring and 11 of row code. Nonblocking: the structured ArchiveOperatorView is now consumed only by the log projection, so the two-step shape (state, then line) carries a little more type than the line needs; keeping it is reasonable while the restart transition is designed, since that transition will want the typed row.\n\nResidual notes for the planner's deploy, not defects of this head:\n- The line is emitted once per object activation. After the deploy, each object emits it when it next activates; the deploy's turnover does that for live objects within a few minutes, so open the tail before deploying and expect one line per object, not a stream.\n- The first call into each activation waits for one getAlarm before its work. That is the accepted warm boundary; it is small, and a failed read or log never refuses the call.\n- Decision 52d805cb's remaining condition is met: no RPC with no hosted caller remains.\n- As for every deploy from Main now: hosted MCP stays closed on the Worker unless MCP_ENABLED is set in the same step (decision 10f45efc).\n\nFindings: none blocking. No hosted mapping, recovery, reset or deployment claim is made or approved here.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a32c0d9eef1a45fe377936dfa5874ed7bc9d14d5","binding":"assigned","head":"a99c2e6742012e2b51968725f11b43cdc5d99964","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6a3f292a9d640d9195385b92dc08947310f939e8\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:352e2c8035dfdd026dd2dbfa17534a7998aaf6db\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4b7d360e6b7bbfa29c8ba36a94154fcf4f449965","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}